h the target variable, as applicants across different regions can have similar financial profiles and behaviors.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n2. Drop `DriversLicense`.\n```python\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\n# While having a driver's license might indicate independence or mobility, it may not bear a strong relationship to credit card approval. It overlaps with demographic and employment features already present (e.g., Age, Employed, Married).\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n3. Drop `BankCustomer`.\n```python\n# Explanation why the column BankCustomer is dropped\n# The BankCustomer feature has a high correlation with the feature Married (both have similar distributions: Q1, Median, Q3). Additionally, theoretical redundancy exists as most working individuals are likely bank customers. More informative features, like Income, provide better insights.\ndf.drop(columns=['BankCustomer'], inplace=True)\n```end", "```python\n# Step 1: Derive Credit-to-Income ratio\ndf['CreditToIncomeRatio'] = df['CreditScore'] / (df['Income'] + 1e-5)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero\n# Feature description: The ratio of someone's credit score to their income.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the relative burden or weight of an individual's credit score compared to their income. This could be a useful indicator in credit risk analysis, where a high credit score relative to income might signal greater ability to repay debts.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: Engineer Age bins (categorical representation)\ndf['AgeBin'] = pd.cut(df['Age'], bins=[0, 25, 35, 50, 75], labels=['<25', '25-35', '35-50', '50+'])\n# Feature description: Binning ages into categories for simpler segmentation.\n# Usefulness: Certain age groups may have distinct financial behaviors or debt-repayment capacities that influence credit card application success probabilities. Capturing this behavior through categories can help 